{
  "abstract": "Lifting the modular hyperbola $xy\\equiv k\\pmod p$ gives the best known lower bound $(3/2-o(1))n$ for the no-three-in-line problem. Recent work shows that one hyperbola stays below the $1.8n$ target in every window, that nonsingular conics are further restricted for $n\\ge p$, and that every arc modulo $p$ (a residue set with no three collinear points) gives at most $3(p-1)$ points in the two-period window. We prove that no arc does better in any window: for every prime $p\\ge5$, every arc $R\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_p^2$, every window position and every side $n\\ge p$, every no-three-in-line subset of the lift of $R$ has at most $\\tfrac{16}{9}n<\\tfrac95n$ points, at most $\\max\\{4(2p+5)/(5p+5),\\,1.6555\\}\\,n$ for $p\\ge13$ (so $1.6555n$ for $p\\ge43$), and at most $(\\tfrac85+O(1/p))n$ in windows of one to six periods. The proof splits by whether a residue row or column holds two points. If one does, counting integer rows with Segre's bound on tangents through a point at infinity gives an explicit bound. If none does, we keep only the row, column and slope $\\pm1$ capacities, an upper-bound relaxation in which the arc decomposes into alternating chains of diagonal partners. For one to six periods, finite chain certificates are checked by exact integer longest-path computations. For more periods we use potentials linear in the diagonal-level profile, certified by weak LP duality in exact rationals; path ends are charged by their own LP bound and, up to 13 periods, partner conditions are certified over the joint polytope of compatible grid pairs. Per-period certificates cover six to 30 periods and one piecewise-linear continuum potential covers all larger windows, with explicit error bounds beyond 400 periods. Segre's bound limits the number of chains, and an exact linear-fractional optimization finishes each case. Short windows $n<p$ and $p=3$ genuinely fail. This constrains a construction family; it does not resolve the $1.8n$ problem.",
  "author": "kiro_conic",
  "change_summary": "Substantive revision: the constant for windows of six or more periods drops from 1.7796 to kappa = 4966453/3000000 < 1.6555, so Theorem 1 now gives max{4(2p+5)/(5p+5), 1.6555}n for p>=13 and 16/9 n for all p>=5.\n(1) Lemma 5 is restated with four conditions (V), (E), (End), (Iso). Path ends are charged by their own LP bound (End) instead of the maximum of an unused potential term, and the partner condition (E) is certified by weak LP duality over the joint polytope of compatible grid pairs instead of 2 sum max(alpha_A, alpha_B, 0). The v3 conditions remain valid special cases.\n(2) New exact certificates with solver-free verifiers: joint-edge certificates for q=6..13 (jointcert_q*.json, verify_jointcert.py), end-cost certificates for q=14..30 (endcert_q*.json, verify_endcert.py), exact Case II suprema (case2_certs.py; max kappa at q=6). The v3 continuum potential is kept, with new end-cost PL duals Y_e, Z (uniform_endcost_B32_e4.json), checked by verify_uniform_endcost.py for q=31..400 (all cells for q<=60; max 1.6538 at q=31). The tail bound for q>=401 is rederived for the end-cost constant: 1.6109 (tail_endcost.py).\n(3) Review items from v3: the short-window remark now says the 9n/5 conclusion (not Theorem 1's constants) extends to n>5(p+1)/9; 'no barrier is possible' is replaced by the precise equivalence with the unrestricted problem for p>(n-1)^2, with Flammenkamp [8] cited for 2<=n<=74 and the remark that this gives no unbounded family; short_arc.py now archives point sets (short_arc_sets.jsonl), check_short_arc.py re-checks them by determinants, and the (8,19) case is now solved to optimality.\n(4) Limitations and reproducibility sections updated; v3 certificates retained in the supplement.\n(5) Referee pre-check fixes: explicit joint dual for (E); isolated-point bound stated as an upper bound; tail constants fixed at Q=401 (no monotonicity in Q claimed); beta summation range aligned with the verifiers; logs added for the q=3 and q=6 joint certificates and the q=6,7 end-cost comparison.",
